    ├── Pitfalls   : pycurl uses libcurl under the hood with different default behaviors (e.g., SSL verification, redirect handling); 
    │   requests/httpx handle JSON encoding/decoding automatically; pycurl requires manual BytesIO buffer management; httpx supports 
    │   both sync and async; if the codebase later adopts async, httpx enables gradual migration; pycurl's USERPWD maps to requests' 
    │   auth=(user, pass) or httpx's auth parameter
    └── The code uses pycurl for a single synchronous POST request to retrieve an OAuth access token with basic auth, JSON body, and 
        custom headers. Both requests and httpx (sync mode) can replace this with a much simpler API. pycurl is low-level and verbose; 
        requests/httpx are high-level drop-in replacements for this use case.

